	abstract = {Parabolic trough solar collectors (PTSC) are widely used in concentrated solar power systems, but their performance is limited at high temperatures. To address these problems, this study systematically investigates heat transfer enhancement for PTSC systems by coupling static mixers with dimpled tubes and nanofluids, aiming to improve thermal efficiency, cost-effectiveness, and environmental sustainability. Heat transfer performance of six nanofluids was investigated at 0.02–0.1 vol% concentration ( ϕ ). These nanofluids were combined with four configurations under Reynolds number ( Re ) from 5000 to 25,000 to conduct a 4E analysis on the PTSC system. The results revealed that MXene nanofluids in the DT-IKSM achieve the optimal performance, which enhances convective heat transfer coefficient ( h ), energy efficiency ( η <sub>en</sub>), and exergy efficiency ( η <sub>ex</sub>) by 5.17 %, 7.06 %, and 2.4 %, respectively, compared to the baseline. Furthermore, a Random Forest model coupled with Non-dominated Sorting Genetic Algorithm II (NSGA-II) was developed for multi-objective optimization, achieving high prediction accuracy and successfully identifying optimal design parameters. The economic analysis demonstrated significant cost savings of approximately 153.9–156.18 USD per collector unit, while environmental assessment indicated approximately 8.506–8.981 % CO<sub>2</sub> emissions reduction per hour. These findings could provide valuable insights for further development of high-efficiency PTSC with substantial potential for industrial-scale solar thermal applications. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd.},
