	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031917304?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Vehicle-to-grid (V2G) technology enables bidirectional energy exchange between electric vehicles (EVs) and the power grid, supporting grid stability, peak load reduction, and operational efficiency. However, existing optimization methods typically rely on day-ahead data and scheduling to address operational uncertainties, which limits their responsiveness to real-time system dynamics and individual-level decisions. This paper proposes a machine learning-based framework trained on historical optimal scheduling decisions under various scenarios to approximate co-optimized outcomes in real time. Specifically, the model predicts whether an EV owner's submitted offer for discharging back to the grid - defined by energy quantity and price - will be accepted based on current system conditions such as demand, generation availability, and market prices. If the offer is accepted, the model also estimates the financial benefit to the EV owner. The framework employs algorithms including random forest, logistic regression, and linear regression, and is benchmarked against a Mixed-Integer Linear Programming (MILP) model. Results demonstrate high predictive accuracy, showing that the proposed method effectively supports real-time decision-making for both grid operators and EV owners, enabling scalable and economically attractive V2G participation.  © 2025 IEEE.},
