	abstract = {The distribution network faces significant challenges, particularly power losses that affect system reliability. Reconfiguring the network by altering the layout through opening and closing switches is a solution to reduce these losses and improve energy distribution reliability. This strategy increases power delivery efficiency and enhances service to consumers. With the transition towards smart grids integrating renewable energy and storage, dynamic network modelling becomes more complex, requiring advanced management solutions. To address this, the research combines Selective Particle Swarm Optimization (SPSO) with the Extra Trees Classifier, a machine learning algorithm based on deep learning, to optimize dynamic distribution network reconfiguration. SPSO is used to identify the optimal network reconfiguration, while the Extra Trees Classifier accelerates computation time and predicts optimal solutions with greater accuracy. This combination enhances both the speed and accuracy of the reconfiguration process. The method also considers isolating faulty branches and reinstalling the network to minimize switching operations and load shedding. Furthermore, it seeks to balance power generation and load demand. The study focuses on minimizing the number of switching operations. The results successfully demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed approach through simulations on the IEEE 33-bus distribution test system. SPSO revealed a 78% reduction in average active power losses and a 48% reduction in reactive power losses following reconfiguration while ETC’s ability to enhance reconfiguration performance, achieving a 0.01669 s reduction in computation time. In comparison, the ETC outperforms other machine learning method such as ANN in accuracy which shows that ETC has better accuracy than ANN. These findings underscore ETC’s advantage in efficiency and precision for reconfiguration tasks. © This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution