	abstract = {Energy management in residential homes has become increasingly critical due to rising energy consumption and the integration of smart grid technologies. Accurate forecasting of household energy usage is essential for optimizing energy consumption, reducing costs, and enhancing grid stability. This paper proposes a novel Adaptive Learning Rate eXtreme Gradient Boosting (ALR-XGBoost) model for home energy forecasting, which dynamically adjusts learning rates to improve convergence. Unlike conventional XGBoost, which relies on a fixed learning rate, our approach modifies the learning rate for each sample based on the gradient (first-order derivative) and boosting iteration to accelerate convergence and prevent overfitting. The model is evaluated on real-world home energy datasets, incorporating solar generation data, enhancing prediction accuracy, and making it particularly useful for smart home energy management and demand response strategies. Experimental results demonstrate that ALR-XGBoost outperforms traditional methods, achieving lower error in Mean Squared Error (MSE), Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE), and Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) while improving the Coefficient of Determination (R2) and enhancing generalization in forecasting dynamic energy patterns. By integrating sample-wise adaptive learning rates, our method improves the robustness of energy consumption forecasting, making it particularly valuable for smart home applications, demand response optimization, and sustainable energy management. This research advances energy analytics by enhancing the adaptability of machine learning models for forecasting fluctuating energy patterns in solar-integrated homes.  © 2025 IEEE.},
