	abstract = {Low-voltage distribution networks face complex load and environmental variations, where current anomaly detection exhibits characteristics such as strong concealment and diverse patterns. Traditional threshold-based and deep learning methods have limitations in detection accuracy, false alarm rates, and deployment efficiency. Therefore, there is an urgent need for a high-precision, low-false-alarm anomaly detection method suitable for edge deployment to ensure the safe and stable operation of power grids. Therefore, this paper proposes a current anomaly detection method for low-voltage distribution networks based on a Bayesian optimization and improved Gradient Boosting Trees (GBDT) fusion framework. Bayesian optimization automatically searches for optimal feature subsets, while the CPEI adaptive segmentation strategy enhances the isolation capability of complex anomalies in the improved isolated forest. Concurrently, GBDT performs fine-grained discrimination to ensure detection accuracy. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed method achieves an average AUC of 0.968 across multiple datasets, representing approximately a 10 % improvement over traditional methods, with a false alarm rate below 2 %. This approach satisfies the practical requirements of low-voltage distribution networks for high accuracy, low false alarms, strong interpretability, and edge-friendly operation, providing an efficient and practical solution for current anomaly detection. © 2025 IEEE.},
