	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105030082732?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Mass applications of Electric Vehicle (EV) necessitate the prediction of energy consumption during charging to prevent critical concerns regarding grid stability and infrastructure's optimisation, and enable renewable integration in the grid. This paper presents a machine learning based aggregate energy consumption predictor for single EV charge sessions with accuracy. Our strategy differs in two respects: first, in the use of a significant EV charge occasions dataset with varying diversity and the use of practice of wide-ranging feature engineering; and second, in the invention of new temporally biased indicators, i.e., day of the week and part of the day, and the use of advanced interaction features of the form charge-distance interaction, kWh-time interaction, and kWh-dollars ratio, which account for nonlinear inter-prts of EV charge inter-action omitted otherwise. After a complete comparative investigation with a Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP), a Support Vector Regressor (SVR), and a specially tuned Random Forest Regressor, the results indicate the specially tuned Random Forest Regressor dominating the rest of the state-of-the-art contenders in accuracy. Our tuned Random Forest-based estimator captures a larger $R^{2}$ with a score of 0.765. This capability of explanation confirms the reasonableness of the model and its applicability in real-world scenarios. The findings of the research are of great significance for influential stakeholders like grid managers, energy suppliers, and urban planners. They enable the availability of data for the application of innovative charging policies with a proactive approach, optimization of grid load management, and acceleration of the shift toward a clean energy system. The study is not just the explanation of the rationale of the sound forecasting methodology, but also reaffirms the new field of AI-based energy management.  © 2025 IEEE.},
