	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105016578605?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This project used a random forest regressor machine learning model to predict the cost of energy in Texas given different energy generation conditions on an hourly basis. The potential energy sources considered include solar, wind, gas, nuclear, hydropower, and coal. We also accounted for both time of day, year, and demand as potential determining factors to consumer cost. The machine learning model takes in rows of data and creates a mathematical equation that best fits the dependent and independent variables, so it can predict the expected price. The purpose of this project is to develop a way to perform prediction calculations at a faster rate. Doing this calculation process with a typical mathematical model takes approximately 5 minutes to calculate, which is not efficient for a large-scale use which requires a unique calculation for each generation scenario. This project could also support the implementation of more nuclear energy on the Texas power grid by displaying the capability of nuclear power to take over the baseload power burden previously put on coal plants. During events like Winter Storm Uri in 2021, blackouts could have potentially been avoided with a different source of baseload power that wasn’t affected by temperature or weather conditions. Also, being able to predict consumer costs can help utilities and policy makers better stabilize prices for a more efficient use of resources and a minimization of risk. First, we compiled the data from GenX, a software developed by the Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT) and Princeton University Zero laboratory. GenX was adapted for Texas Grid purposes by the Webber Energy Group at the University of Texas at Austin. Drawing from several of these simulations we made a file with over 78-thousand-hour time steps of generation scenarios to run our algorithm on. Each generation scenario is some combination of the energy in megawatts that is produced by different technologies in Texas. Using Python, we cleaned the 78-thousand steps of any nonnumeric values or inconsistencies. We chose the random forest regressor from scikit-learn, developed by David Cournapeau, after exploring other models because it best captured the complex relationships within the data. A random forest regressor is an ensemble learning method that combines multiple decision trees to improve prediction accuracy and handle complex, nonlinear relationships in data. Unlike a single decision tree, it reduces overfitting by averaging multiple models, making it more robust and reliable. After training the algorithm on a random 90 percent of the data and testing on a randomized 10 percent we received an accuracy score of 75 percent. We attempted to improve the score by adding new variables such as time of day and season. This data still needs refinement and will be investigated in future work. The future potential of this project is to develop an online resource for consumers and policy makers to have access to reliable and accurate energy cost information for an infinite number of generation scenarios on the Texas grid. © 2025, American Nuclear Society. All rights reserved.},
