	abstract = {Model predictive control (MPC) is a well-known approach to operate building energy systems (BESs) efficiently. However, MPC is not widely adopted in practice. The reasons for this are, inter alia, high data infrastructure requirements and missing know-how among practitioners. Consequently, rule-based controllers are still most common in BESs. In order to achieve a simple yet almost optimal operation, approximate MPC is a promising approach. Here, the outputs of an optimization-based controller are imitated by extracted rules which can also be executed offline, resulting in simple applicability and reduced computational effort. In this context, heat pump (HP) systems have not been thoroughly assessed yet, even though they will play a crucial role in future BESs and their operation optimization is promising. In addition, storage operation optimization based on approximated operating rules has also not been investigated widely. Therefore, we close this gap by developing an approximate, open-loop optimal control approach for a HP system with a hot and cold thermal energy storage (TES, CTES) and an ice storage. The approximate optimal control is based on optimization results of a mixed integer linear program (MILP), which minimizes the system’s operating costs by determining the optimal operating mode schedule. Based on the optimization results, we extract rules for the offline controller using a Random Forest (RF) Classifier. In addition, we introduce the rules’ comprehensibility as key performance indicator (KPI), which is often neglected in literature. Therefore, we quantify the influence of a change in RF specific parameters, which lead to more comprehensible rules, on the method’s performance. Results prove that the developed method succeeds in imitating the optimal mode schedule. However, a trade-off exists between the RF’s complexity and its performance. For example, a tree with a maximum depth of 30 nodes results in an R<sup>2</sup> of 1.0, while an 